prompt:
Can you tell me what's wrong with the fonts on our clinic's site? Patients say it looks messy but I can't tell why. Here's the CSS:

body { font-family: 'Open Sans', sans-serif; }
h1 { font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif; }
h2 { font-family: 'Montserrat', sans-serif; }
h3, .doctor-name { font-family: 'Lora', serif; }
.btn, nav a { font-family: 'Poppins', sans-serif; }
.phone-number { font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; }

The clinic is Harbor Family Medicine, mostly older patients, lots of appointment info.

Quick sanity check before I ship this. It's the font setup for the Tidewater Almanac, a small newsletter site about coastal weather and tides. Anything you'd change?

:root {
  --serif: Charter, 'Bitstream Charter', 'Sitka Text', Cambria, serif;
  --sans: system-ui, -apple-system, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, sans-serif;
}
body { font-family: var(--serif); }
h1, h2, h3 { font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 700; }
.tide-table, .meta { font-family: var(--sans); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }

Body is long-form articles plus a daily tide table.

Our pricing table looks jumpy and I can't figure out why, the prices don't seem to line up between rows. We use a nice humanist sans and I like it, so I don't want to change the look. Here's the relevant CSS:

.plan-table { font-family: 'Gill Sans', 'Gill Sans MT', Calibri, sans-serif; font-size: 18px; }
.plan-table td.price { text-align: right; font-weight: 600; }
.plan-table td.seats { text-align: right; }

Rows look like: Starter $9 / 3 seats, Team $49 / 25 seats, Business $119 / 100 seats, Enterprise $1,240 / 1,000 seats. How do I fix it?
